{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nSplit out of `d26a-8ffa-97cd-4b2a` (`dire-expectable-terrapin`), where these were carried as\nits DEFERRED AC5 and AC6. That bug fixed the key-path DIVERGENCE that made the deployed MCP\nserver sign op-certs it could never verify (Gerrit 2337, merged as\n`83edf9972728ae1e0729a39098ec70e4fab4617a`): verification now resolves the public half from the\nsame ordered chain the signing side resolves the private half.\n\nWhat that fix did NOT add is any GUARD. `sign_manifest` still mints a certificate without\nchecking that the environment can resolve a public counterpart for the key it just signed\nunder, and nothing at deploy/startup surfaces it when it cannot. So the CLASS of defect — a\nsilently uncertifiable attestation — can regress the moment a new deployment shape puts the\nsigning key somewhere the resolution chain does not reach. Signing succeeds; only a later\n`verify_signature` (or a refused claim) reveals the problem, potentially long after the\ncertificates were minted.\n\n## Approach\nTwo independent, additive guards, neither of which changes an existing success path:\n\n1. At MINT: after `ensure_opcert_key` resolves the SPECIFIC signing key path used for this\n   mint, read THAT key's own public line with `_read_opcert_pub(key_path)` and check whether it\n   is a MEMBER of the verify-side resolvable set `_opcert_own_public_keys`\n   (`src/rebar/_opcert_signing.py`). Matching is on the key TYPE+BASE64 BODY (comment-\n   insensitive), mirroring ssh `allowed_signers`. The check is membership of the specific\n   signing key's public counterpart, NOT merely the non-emptiness of the aggregate chain — a\n   chain where a DIFFERENT key resolves a pub must NOT satisfy the guard for the key actually\n   signed under. When the specific key's counterpart is absent, emit an explicit WARNING naming\n   the resolved key path and the principal. It must NOT refuse — refusing would break every\n   existing sign flow and is a behaviour change beyond this bug's scope.\n\n   Worked example (specification by example). Own-key chain = [binding-key `K_b`, genesis\n   `K_g`]. `ensure_opcert_key` selects `K_g` for this mint (binding absent), but only `K_b`'s\n   `.pub` is derivable (K_g lives on a path the resolver cannot reach). Aggregate\n   `_opcert_own_public_keys` = {body(K_b)} — NON-EMPTY. A non-emptiness check would stay\n   SILENT (wrong). The specified membership check computes `body(_read_opcert_pub(path_of\n   K_g))` ∉ {body(K_b)} → WARNS (correct), naming `path_of K_g` and the principal.\n\n2. At STARTUP/DEPLOY: the MCP server's existing health surface (`src/rebar/_mcp_health.py`)\n   reports a DEGRADED field and logs a boot WARNING when the bound startup signer's public key\n   is not the pinned trusted-environment key for its principal — the ONE same-environment\n   failure the (derivative) public key cannot otherwise catch. SERVE-DEGRADED, never abort\n   boot (operator ruling; ADR 0104 decision 3 makes required-environment binding advisory\n   today), mirroring `store_status` / `run_startup_store_sweep`.\n\nBoth are in-process checks driven by an operation and by server startup, with no dependency on\nany particular CI provider.\n\n### AC3 reframing (operator ruling, recorded here)\nThe op-cert public key is DERIVATIVE (`_derive_opcert_pub` = `ssh-keygen -y`, re-derivable on\ndemand from the private key), and `compose_startup_opcert_binding` already fails closed on a\nbad/missing/encrypted/non-Ed25519 private key. So a \"can we resolve/derive a public cert?\"\nstartup probe would be REDUNDANT. The one genuinely non-redundant condition is whether the\nbound signer's public key MATCHES the environment's pinned/published trusted-environment key\n(`rebar.attest.trusted_env`) — the key required-environment verify checks against. Even that is\nnon-blocking: those are still valid signed attestations that only fail an advisory\nenvironment-binding check, so AC3 emits a degraded health field + a warning and does NOT refuse\nto serve or abort boot.\n\n## Scope\n- In scope: the mint-time warning and the startup/health serve-degraded surface.\n- Out of scope: the key-resolution chain itself (already fixed by 2337); any change to the\n  cross-environment `foreign_key` verdict, which is documented intended behaviour (ADR 0104),\n  not a defect; extending `compose_startup_opcert_binding` to abort; a redundant derive probe.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tests: a mint under a key whose SPECIFIC public half cannot be resolved (even when another\nchain key's pub resolves) emits the warning and still returns a signature; a mint under a\nresolvable key emits no warning; the startup/health check reports degraded (and warns) for a\nbound signer whose public key is not the pinned key, and is quiet/non-degraded for a matched\nkey, for an unbound signer, and for a deployment that has not opted into pinning; key matching\nis on the key type+base64 body (comment-insensitive); a resolution fault is surfaced as\ndegraded (not swallowed); a `RemovedInputError` fails hard.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] AC1: `sign_manifest` emits an explicit WARNING — naming the resolved signing key path and the principal — when the SPECIFIC key it just signed under has no public counterpart in the verify-side resolvable set (`_read_opcert_pub(key_path)` body ∉ `_opcert_own_public_keys`), even if a different chain key's pub resolves; it still returns the signature (no refusal, no existing sign flow broken).\n- [x] AC2: `sign_manifest` emits no such warning when the specific signing key's public counterpart IS resolvable.\n- [x] AC3: the MCP server's startup/health surface reports a DEGRADED `opcert` field and logs a boot WARNING when the bound signer's public key is not the pinned trusted-environment key for its principal, and is non-degraded/quiet when it matches (or when unbound / pinning not configured). Non-blocking: it NEVER aborts boot (serve-degraded; ADR 0104 decision 3).\n- [x] AC4: both guards are in-process and operation-linked, with no dependency on a specific CI provider.\n\n## Definition of Done\n- [x] DD1: AC1 and AC2 covered by a test that is RED before the change and GREEN after it, including the specific-key case where a different chain key's pub resolves (aggregate non-empty) yet the signed key's own counterpart is absent.\n- [x] DD2: AC3 covered by a test over the startup/health check (degraded, matched, unbound, and not-pinned cases).\n- [x] DD3: AC4 verified by the observable proxy that both guards execute with no CI-provider environment present — the unit tests exercise mint and startup/health with no CI env vars set and assert the warning/degraded behaviour purely from in-process log/dict output (no network, no CI API), confirming operation/startup-linked triggers.\n\n## Verification evidence (Phase 2, landed as Gerrit 2360 patchset 7 — MERGED)\n- AC1/AC2 (mint warning) & AC3 (serve-degraded /health opcert field + boot warning): implemented as `_warn_if_verify_cannot_resolve` in `mint_opcert_record` (`_opcert_signing.py`) and `opcert_signing_status`/`opcert_signer_degraded`/`run_startup_opcert_check` (`_mcp_opcert_health.py`, surfaced via `_mcp_health.py` `/health` + `run_mcp`). AC4: both guards are in-process/operation-linked (mint path + server startup), no CI-provider dependency.\n- Held-out RED→GREEN test `tests/unit/test_opcert_public_counterpart_guard_879b.py` (12 tests) covers DD1 (AC1/AC2 incl. the specific-key case where a different chain key's pub resolves) and DD2 (AC3 degraded/matched/unbound/not-pinned), asserting observable log/dict output with no CI env set (DD3/AC4).\n- Mutation proof (sandboxed): neuter the mint guard → 2 RED (AC1/AC2); force `opcert_signer_degraded`→False → 3 RED (AC3); restore → 12 GREEN.\n- CI (Gerrit Verified gate, GitHub Actions run 33265621222): all pytest legs (ubuntu py3.11/3.12/3.13, macos py3.13) + lint/typecheck/mutation/optionality GREEN → Verified +1; LLM-Review +1. Change MERGED to origin/main.\n- Module-size compliance: the rebase onto a grown origin/main tipped `_mcp_health.py` and `_opcert_signing.py` over the 800-LOC cap; resolved by extracting `_mcp_opcert_health.py` (115 LOC) and `_opcert_verify.py` (340 LOC) along existing call-graph seams (all `src/rebar/*.py` ≤ 800; complexity baseline new=0/increased=0)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788026126704498001,"uuid":"f5fc27ec-a6f3-4f32-b069-2bc2a66cb0e8"}